    <h2>Why our bread takes two days</h2>
    <p>
        Every loaf starts the afternoon before you buy it. The dough is mixed with our levain, the starter Hattie has kept alive since 2012,
        folded by hand four times over the afternoon, shaped, and left in the walk-in cooler overnight. Cold slows the yeast and lets the
        bacteria get on with the flavor: that gentle sourness, the nutty depth, the blistered crust.
    </p>
    <p>
        At four the next morning the first loaves go into the deck oven, and we bake them dark. A pale loaf is an unfinished loaf; the
        color on the crust is where most of the flavor is.
    </p>

    <h2>The flour</h2>
    <p>
        Most of our flour is milled in the state, and a third of it is whole grain: wheat, rye and spelt, stone-ground so it keeps its germ and
        its taste. It drinks more water than supermarket flour, which is why our crumb is open and custardy.
    </p>

    <h2>When it comes out of the oven</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>First bake, 6:30am: country loaves, rye, tins and the morning baguettes.</li>
        <li>Second bake, 10am: fougasse, specials, and a second round of country.</li>
        <li>Afternoon baguettes, 1pm: for supper, still crackling at three.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
